---
title: Check a loose shutter
lang: en
---

## Start with the record

By the end of this lesson, you can choose a first check for a loose shutter and record what that check establishes.

A shutter at Sedge Gate slips after Mira lets go. The route ledger still records amber from the previous keeper's visit. Start by observing whether the panel stays in place. Do not rewrite the old visit to make it agree with the new one. Each visit needs its own entry.

A [slip check]{A check of whether the shutter stays where the keeper leaves it.} establishes position, not the reason a fastening failed.

--- Separate the checks

## Separate the checks

### Position check
as: accordion

Move the demonstration panel, let go and observe where it settles. Record whether it stays or slips.

### Fastening note

Describe the visible fastening without claiming it caused the slip. A loose pin is a detail to examine.

### Keeper reply

Ask the previous keeper what they observed during their visit. Keep their reply separate from your own check.

close: accordion

When the panel returns slowly enough that the movement looks finished until you look away, and the route ledger still contains a confident amber entry from an earlier visit, repeat the position check while a second keeper watches the lower edge and records where it finally settles.

--- Select the first check

## Select the first check

The panel moves after you release it. You have not yet inspected the fastening or spoken to the earlier keeper.

### What should your first new entry describe?
as: choice

(x) Whether the panel stays or slips after release
feedback: This records the result of the position check you can observe.
( ) Why the earlier keeper missed the fault
feedback: You have not asked what that keeper observed.
( ) The name of the failed fastening
feedback: You have not established which fastening failed.

--- Plan a second visit

## Plan a second visit

Write two questions for a second keeper who will visit Sedge Gate after you. One should ask about the panel's position. The other should ask for information your first check could not establish.

Put each question beside the ledger detail that prompted it. Leave room for the second keeper's reply. This worksheet has no answer key because several useful questions may follow from the same observation.

Explain which new information would make you revise your interpretation.
